\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Fix $0<\epsilon<1$. If $A\subset\mathbb F_2^n$ and $V\leq\mathbb F_2^n$ is a linear subspace, put $\alpha_A(V)=|A\cap V|/|V|$. Call $V$ $\epsilon$-stable for $A$ if
\[
 |\alpha_A(V)-\alpha_A(V')|\leq\epsilon
 \quad\text{for every linear }V'\leq V\text{ with }\dim V'=\dim V-1.
\]
The zero subspace is stable vacuously. Write $\operatorname{codim}(V)=n-\dim V$ and define the optimal uniform bound
\[
 D(\epsilon)=\sup_{n\geq1}\ \sup_{A\subset\mathbb F_2^n}\
 \min\{\operatorname{codim}(V):V\text{ is }\epsilon\text{-stable for }A\}.
\]
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Obtain sharp bounds for $D(\epsilon)$ as $\epsilon\downarrow0$: how small a codimension, uniformly in $n$ and $A$, suffices to find a subspace whose density changes by at most $\epsilon$ on every codimension-one subspace? The source records an exponential-in-$1/\epsilon$ upper bound and power-of-$1/\epsilon$ lower examples; the question is to determine the correct quantitative dependence. \href{https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine how small a codimension guarantees a linear subspace on which the density of any given binary-vector-space set is stable under passing to every hyperplane.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 143 (GREEN-055). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Ben Green, \emph{100 Open Problems}, maintained author collection, Problem 89 and its comments, accessed 7 October 2026. \url{https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf}.
\end{description}
